#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162848} :activity/id #uuid "a251e410-6f92-5d37-91f6-3fe7f0377f20" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050539 :topic/id #uuid "52089b25-b5c5-5717-906a-bd765cce0502" :topic/math-academy-id 3119} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162848 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163223 :tutorial/content "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a real matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nThe procedure for finding an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3 \\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ is similar to diagonalizing a regular (diagonalizable) $3\\times 3$ matrix:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of *unit orthogonal* eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nAs an example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. So let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.**  We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\qquad \\lambda_2=-6, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-9.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** We need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Seeking a nonzero solution of $|A-\\lambda_i I| = 0$ for $i=1,2,3,$ we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that the eigenvectors are mutually orthogonal. \nNow, an orthogonal diagonalization requires each eigenvector to be normalized. So, dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the following unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** In this example, we automatically get orthogonal eigenvectors because $A$ has three *distinct* eigenvalues. However, in the case of repeated eigenvalues, the corresponding eigenvectors might not be orthogonal." :tutorial/id #uuid "0ff932ad-00a4-58cd-952f-55c21e02ef43" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4881 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0a486be1-0f5d-50ea-a245-3aedf2c597b3" :step/math-academy-id 30532 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162872}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161988 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161780 :question/id #uuid "054b9992-0bf6-5dcc-a04b-038ad6559959" :question/math-academy-id "e-8172"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "611af093-6621-5224-af2a-f769c7c0e691" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394963}] :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "34cd3b8c-2df0-5886-a6f1-17125ede8748" :step/math-academy-id 30594 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162915}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162871 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161989 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161781 :question/id #uuid "f6714653-0cfa-5049-9ae9-f921d46d03bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8173"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b16e6cc-b1ee-5ccc-91a7-980bf5fe50a5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050578}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404700}] :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "e9df74bd-9e6b-5113-a23f-86c30ccc850b" :step/math-academy-id 30595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162872 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161990 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161782 :question/id #uuid "4779b6be-97c9-5389-b25e-e8f438f0a7fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8175"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77a2b06b-e239-59a5-8af0-6cbbf27c3c08" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048493}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364523}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"} :step/id #uuid "f9795272-744c-5dd3-afc9-73a980ee4bf9" :step/math-academy-id 30597 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163249 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the algorithm for diagonalizing a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A.$\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of unit orthogonal eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nNote the following important points regarding step 2 of this process:\n\n- If $A$ has mutually distinct eigenvalues, the eigenvectors will automatically be orthogonal, so we only need to normalize them.\n\n- If $A$ has *repeated* eigenvalues, then the eigenvectors corresponding to these repeated eigenvalues *may not* be orthogonal. Therefore, we have to apply the Gram-Schmidt process to obtain orthogonal vectors.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. Let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.**  We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4, \\qquad \\lambda_2=4, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Seeking a nonzero solution of $|A-\\lambda_i I| = 0$ for $i=1,2,3,$ we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ form an *orthogonal* basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ad655c9-a00e-5174-a98f-6ae8ab07843d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5013 :tutorial/title "Symmetric Matrices With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "63db5c7a-ccd4-5948-af8b-421d4a60d2da" :step/math-academy-id 31356 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162871}}] :activity/title "Diagonalization of 3x3 Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162215}]}
